{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789397646,"nonce":"lQGndxutGxBHmR/kDbsoSQjdLdA=","title":"Releasing a list header leaks its element storage","message":"A growable list is two allocations: the `{data, len, capacity}` header\nand the element buffer the header's first field points at. The drop\npass released the header with a bare free and never touched the buffer,\nbecause the buffer's own allocation site \"escapes\" (it is stored into\nthe header) and a header of an anonymous three-word struct told the\npass nothing about what it owns.\n\nReproducer (pressure/lists.py): `xs = [i, i + 1, i + 2]; xs.append(i + 3)`\nin a loop grew by about 30 bytes a step, 27 MB at 2,000 steps to 88 MB\nat 2,000,000, with the header freed and the 32-byte buffer left.\n\nFix: the heap header carries the `List` name (`ssa::list_header_type`),\n`release_for` gives such a site `Release::List`, whose emission loads\nthe buffer out of the header and frees it before the header. So that a\npointer read out of the header extends the header's life and cannot be\nreleased early, the derived-value walk treats a pointer loaded from a\nlist header's first field as a name for the header's storage, a\n`realloc` of it as the same storage moved, and a store of it back into\nthe header as a use rather than an escape. `memcpy`/`memmove`/`memset`\narguments count as uses, since they keep nothing.","files":null}]}